Here’s a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you would invoke the `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` helper to serialize a `RootObjectMapper` that has all the “default” flags turned on (dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection) **while explicitly telling it to skip runtime fields**.

```java
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Create an XContentBuilder instance – this is what will receive
//     the JSON/YAML/… output from the mapper.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
XContentBuilder xContentBuilderInstance = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Build the RootObjectMapper with the desired defaults.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
RootObjectMapper rootMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder()
        // 2a.  Dynamic date formats – use the standard Elasticsearch default
        .dynamicDateFormats(
                Collections.singletonList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis")
        )
        // 2b.  Dynamic templates – leave empty so the mapper will fall back
        //      to the default behaviour (i.e. no custom templates).
        .dynamicTemplates(Collections.emptyList())
        // 2c.  Enable date detection – tells the mapper to look for date‑like
        //      strings when mapping unknown fields.
        .dateDetection(true)
        // 2d.  Enable numeric detection – tells the mapper to look for
        //      numeric values when mapping unknown fields.
        .numericDetection(true)
        .build();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Prepare the parameters that control the serialization.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add("include_defaults");   // Include default values in the output
params.add("skip_runtime_fields"); // Skip runtime fields during serialization

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Call the helper – note that the helper expects *string* arguments
//     because the tool interface works with string representations of
//     Java objects.  In real code you would simply call:
//         rootMapper.doXContent(xContentBuilderInstance, params);
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
String builderStr = "xContentBuilderInstance"; // placeholder string
String paramsStr  = "params";                  // placeholder string

// Invoke the function via the tool interface
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent({
    builder: builderStr,
    params: paramsStr
});
```

### What the code does

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **1️⃣** | Instantiates an `XContentBuilder` that will hold the serialized output. |
| **2️⃣** | Builds a `RootObjectMapper` with the following defaults:<br>- `dynamicDateFormats`: the standard ES format (`strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis`).<br>- `dynamicTemplates`: empty list → no custom templates.<br>- `dateDetection`: enabled.<br>- `numericDetection`: enabled. |
| **3️⃣** | Creates an `ArrayList<Object>` containing two flags that the serializer understands:<br>- `"include_defaults"` tells the mapper to write out default values.<br>- `"skip_runtime_fields"` tells it to ignore runtime fields. |
| **4️⃣** | Calls the helper function. In a normal Java environment you would simply call `rootMapper.doXContent(xContentBuilderInstance, params);`. Because the tool interface requires string representations, we pass `"xContentBuilderInstance"` and `"params"` as placeholders. |

> **Note:**  
> - The actual `doXContent` implementation will read the state of the `rootMapper` instance you built in step 2 and write the corresponding JSON/YAML/etc. into the `XContentBuilder`.  
> - The `params` list is interpreted by the serializer; adding `"include_defaults"` ensures that even default values are emitted, while `"skip_runtime_fields"` guarantees that any runtime fields defined on the mapper are omitted from the output.  

Feel free to replace the placeholder strings with the real variable names if you’re calling the function directly in your own code.

